{"id": "76d9d96b-ffe0-4a90-82e7-3ee0e624f811", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "That skill lives with my research tool, so let me have it pull the CloudTrail coverage details for your account.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_fJeVjntjLRp8Pfe6GpixAf", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Audit CloudTrail coverage for this AWS account (a member account in an AWS Organization). Specifically: (1) identify which part of the account's CloudTrail coverage comes from an organization trail vs. any account-level trail, and (2) assess whether CloudTrail Event history alone (without a trail) would be sufficient for the auditors' needs.",
  "context": "User is an auditor/engineer in a member account of an AWS Organization asking two things: how much of their CloudTrail visibility is inherited from the org trail (vs their own trail, if any), and whether Event history (the default 90-day API-only view) can substitute for a configured trail for audit purposes. Use the cloudtrail-coverage-auditor skill/methodology if available to you.",
  "resources": ["CloudTrail trails in this account", "organization trail (if visible/inherited)", "Event history default behavior"],
  "timeframe": "current state as of now",
  "criteria": "Determine: whether an org trail is applied to this account and what it covers (management events, data events, regions, multi-region); whether there is also a local/account-level trail and what it adds on top of the org trail; concrete gaps if any (e.g., data events for S3/Lambda/DynamoDB not captured); and give a clear verdict with reasoning on whether Event history is adequate for audit purposes (retention window, event types covered, whether it captures data events, whether it's exportable/searchable long-term, whether non-API activity is excluded)."
}", "deep_research": false}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit CloudTrail coverage in this member account, focusing on org trail contribution and Event history reliability."}], "created_at": null}
